#section("I. The Resonance and the Thesis")

It is tempting, and cheap, to hear in "eternal progression" only a rhyme with
Kant's word _eternal_ and stop there. The resonance is deeper than the pun, and
more interesting, because it survives the removal of the shared vocabulary: both
Kant and the Latter-day Saint tradition refuse the picture of salvation as a
single, instantaneous, static event, and both put in its place a picture of
perfection as a _trajectory_ — something one is always on the way toward, never
simply in possession of. The refusal is structural, and it is the same refusal.
What follows from it is not.

This essay argues that the family resemblance is real and locatable — it holds at
two identifiable joints and breaks at three — and that the interesting question is
not whether the two traditions agree that perfection is a process (they do) but
_how confidently each is permitted to say so_, and _whether the gap between the
creature and God ever actually closes_. Kant gives us an epistemically modest,
asymptotic version of eternal progression: an endless approach, never an arrival,
advanced with no claim to know it is true. The Latter-day Saint tradition gives us
an epistemically confident, teleological one: an actual arrival, doctrinally
asserted, with an ontological continuity between the human and the divine. Between
those two lies the whole of the disagreement, and it is worth mapping precisely,
because a resemblance stated loosely flatters both parties and instructs neither.

I proceed in three movements. The first locates where the two accounts genuinely
rhyme — in the asymptotic structure of the moral self, and in the refusal to let
that self be wholly constituted by an external cause. The second locates where they
pull apart, along three axes that are, I will argue, at least as important as the
resemblance. The third offers a compact framing of the whole, and asks what the
comparison teaches about the price each tradition pays for the shape of perfection
it chooses.
